# Session history

Log of work done across sessions. Started 2026-09-14.

## 2026-09-14 — Fix laptop brightness hotkeys (Lenovo LOQ, hybrid graphics)

Compositor: niri (Wayland). Brightness/volume/media keys are bound in
`modules/features/niri.nix` to spawn-sh the wrapped `myNoctalia` shell:
`noctalia ipc call brightness increase|decrease`.

### Symptoms
- Volume keys already worked (via PipeWire, per-user).
- Brightness keys did nothing.

### Diagnosis
- Two backlight devices exist: `acpi_video0` (max 49) and
  `nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight` (max 800). The latter is the real panel control
  (forced on via `boot.extraModprobeConfig = "options nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight force=1"`).
- 2 root causes:
  1. `noctalia.json` (baked into the wrapped noctalia package at build time)
     mapped output `eDP-1` to device `nvidia_ec_backlight` — never existed.
     `brightnessctl -d nvidia_ec_backlight` is a no-op. Real name:
     `nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight`.
  2. `/sys/class/backlight/*/brightness` is `root:root` mode 644 and user
     `riot` is not in the `video` group; no udev rule granted group-write,
     so even the correct device failed with EACCES.

### Changes applied
- `modules/features/noctalia.json`
  - `"device": "nvidia_ec_backlight"` -> `"device": "nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight"`
    (backlightDeviceMappings for output `eDP-1`).
- `modules/hosts/myMachine/configuration.nix`
  - `users.users.riot.extraGroups` now `[ "networkmanager" "wheel" "video" ]`.
  - Added `services.udev.extraRules` to `chgrp video` + `chmod g+w` on every
    `/sys/class/backlight/%k/brightness` on ADD (standard brightnessctl rule).

### How to apply (needs root)
```sh
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /home/riot/.dotfiles#myMachine
sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=backlight
```
Then log out/in of the niri session (applies `video` group; restarts
noctalia so it loads the newly baked config).

### Verification
- `ls -l /sys/class/backlight/*/brightness` -> expect `-rw-rw-r-- 1 root video`.
- Press `XF86MonBrightnessUp/Down` -> screen + noctalia OSD change.

### Notes / gotchas
- The nix flake target is `nixosConfigurations.myMachine` (flake at
  `~/.dotfiles`, flake-parts + import-tree).
- `boot.extraModprobeConfig` with `nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight force=1` was added
  in a prior (uncommitted) edit; it is required for the device to exist.
- The git tree in `~/.dotfiles` has uncommitted changes — commit if desired.
- `noctalia.json` is read by Nix at build time (`builtins.fromJSON`), so
  runtime edits under `~/.config/noctalia` are not what the launcher uses;
  rebuild the package to bake config in.
- Volume worked because PipeWire is per-user; brightness needs sysfs write
  access, hence the udev rule + `video` group.

## 2026-09-14 (later) — Physical brightness STILL not changing; prior fix was a dead end

### Findings (diagnostics during this session)
- Keys fire and noctalia OSD reacts, but the panel never moves physically.
- Root cause: NEITHER sysfs backlight device actually drives the panel:
  - `acpi_video0` (max 49): writes persist (file/actual both update to 12/49)
    but the screen ignores them -> dead ACPI stub for this BIOS/graphics mode.
  - `nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight` (max 800): value accepted, `actual_brightness`
    stays 0, OSD reacts via watchers, but no physical change -> phantom. It is
    force-loaded by nouveau (there IS no proprietary nvidia driver in use).
- Hardware reality:
  - Panel `eDP-1` (1920x1080@144) is wired to the NVIDIA dGPU "card0" (PCI
    0000:01:00.0, driver nouveau, runtime active, 2.5 GT/s).
  - iGPU amdgpu "card1" (PCI 0000:06:00.0/00:08.1) is up but all its outputs
    (incl. `card1-eDP-2`) are disconnected -> dynamic MUX in discrete mode.
  - GPU-native backlight is exposed nowhere in `/sys/class/backlight`.
- udev rule (chgrp video + chmod g+w) never applied at the earlier boot (files
  still 644 root:root), but that's moot since neither device drives the panel.

### Change applied (uncommitted)
- `modules/hosts/myMachine/configuration.nix`
  - REMOVED `boot.extraModprobeConfig = "options nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight force=1"`.
  - ADDED `boot.kernelParams = [ "acpi_backlight=vendor" ]` so the Lenovo
    vendor/ideapad EC backlight takes over from the dead ACPI video stub.

### Next (pending hardware test)
- Rebuild + reboot, confirm `/sys/class/backlight/ideapad` exists and actually
  changes the physical panel.
- If it works: map `eDP-1 -> ideapad` in `modules/features/noctalia.json`,
  rerun udev trigger (or rely on fresh add events), restart noctalia.
- Fallbacks if `ideapad` is also dead: proprietary NVIDIA driver (panel is
  dGPU-driven), or BIOS graphics mode -> iGPU/MShybrid so `amdgpu_bl0` works.

## 2026-09-14 — Step 3 implemented: acpi_backlight=vendor + ideapad backlight WORKS

### Hardware test passed
- `/proc/cmdline` includes `acpi_backlight=vendor` (rebooted with it active).
- `/sys/class/backlight/ideapad` exists (max 11), owned `root:video` mode 664 —
  the udev rule applied at this boot.
- Write test (as user `riot`): wrote 2 -> brightness=2 AND actual_brightness=2;
  restored 1 -> 1/1. Unlike the nvidia phantom (actual stayed 0), this device
  genuinely tracks the panel. Physical screen confirmed moving.

### Changes applied
- `modules/features/noctalia.json`
  - `backlightDeviceMappings`: `"device": "nvidia_wmi_ec_backlight"` ->
    `"device": "ideapad"` for output `eDP-1`.
- Rebuilt package to bake config in (noctalia.json is read by Nix at build time):
  `nix build .#myNoctalia` -> `/nix/store/4yy2fxnwxaqazlsbk7xk8d85j6kjzcab-noctalia-shell-4.7.7`
  - verified `noctalia-shell-config/settings.json` now contains `"ideapad"`.

### Still required (needs root, sudo prompts for password)
The running system closure (`/nix/store/mijlx0qxq...-noctalia-shell-4.7.7`) still has
the old mapping; niri keybinds/spawn-at-startup resolve `myNoctalia` at system build time.
```sh
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /home/riot/.dotfiles#myMachine
```
Then log out/in of niri so noctalia relaunches with the new package. No udev
trigger needed this boot (files already proper). After the switch: keys fire,
OSD reacts, and the panel physically changes.

## 2026-09-25 — Declarative Sweet GTK/Kvantum theme feature

### Scope
- Added `features.themes.enable`, defaulting to false, for native GTK2/GTK3/GTK4
  applications, GNOME application settings, Metacity, and Qt/Kvantum.
- Flatpak configuration was left unchanged; the existing BeautyLine system
  package and `/run/host/share/icons/BeautyLine` exposure remain intact. No
  GNOME Shell theme was added.
- Existing cursor, font, scaling, and unrelated settings are preserved. The
  feature changes only theme, icon, and dark-preference keys.

### Changes
- `flake.nix`: added the non-flake `sweet` input from
  `github:EliverLara/Sweet/nova`.
- `flake.lock`: pinned Sweet to revision
  `46e3802d3f4d9e992f94264f1d834f5f9621c03b` with narHash
  `sha256-rB6l1u12u+X2kgwKUJRLYuBu6Q+cq5/1Arp5dMILYcE=`.
- `modules/features/themes.nix`: packages the pinned Sweet GTK2/GTK3/GTK4 and
  Metacity assets as `Sweet-Dark`, corrects its icon metadata to BeautyLine,
  installs the Sweet Kvantum theme and Qt5/Qt6 Kvantum engines, and provides
  idempotent activation for existing GTK and Kvantum files. The disabled path
  does not manage those files or remove existing dconf values.
- `modules/hosts/myMachine/configuration.nix`: imports the theme module and
  enables `features.themes`.
- Preserved the pre-existing removal of `vscode`, the `jetbrains.jdk`
  replacement, and the `zip`/`unzip` additions.

### Verification
- `nix flake check path:/home/riot/.dotfiles --no-build` passed.
- Nix parsing, targeted nixfmt, and `git diff --check` passed.
- Built the Home Manager profile and activation package; verified Sweet assets,
  Kvantum assets, and both Qt plugin paths in the generated profile.
- Ran the activation script twice against temporary fixtures: unrelated keys
  were preserved and the second run was identical.
- Evaluated the feature-disabled configuration: no theme package, activation
  entry, or theme session variables were emitted.
- Verified BeautyLine is visible inside all three installed Flatpaks; no user
  Flatpak overrides were present.

### Status
- The configuration was built and evaluated but not applied to the live
  session. No commit was made.

## 2026-09-25 — Declarative Sweet Flatpak GTK overrides

### Scope
- Added `features.themes.flatpakApplications`, defaulting to an empty list.
- Configured the installed Flatpaks `com.mattjakeman.ExtensionManager`,
  `io.github.screwys.Rufin`, and `net.xmind.XMind8` for system-wide GTK theming.
- Kept Flatpaks GTK-only; native Qt/Kvantum configuration remains unchanged.

### Changes
- `modules/features/themes.nix`: split the Sweet GTK assets and generated GTK2
  configuration into a reusable `sweet-gtk-theme` bridge while preserving the
  native GTK, Metacity, and Kvantum package behavior.
- Added a stateful system activation that applies only listed application IDs
  with `flatpak override --system`, a read-only GTK asset mount, `GTK_DATA_PREFIX`,
  `GTK_THEME`, and `GTK2_RC_FILES`.
- Added cleanup for removed applications and changed theme store paths without
  resetting unrelated Flatpak override entries.
- `modules/hosts/myMachine/configuration.nix`: added the three application IDs
  to the allowlist.

### Verification
- `nix flake check path:/home/riot/.dotfiles --no-build` passed.
- The NixOS system toplevel, generated activation package, and Sweet packages
  built successfully.
- Verified generated GTK assets and the GTK2 config inside a Flatpak runtime.
- Ran the generated activation script against a fake Flatpak backend for apply,
  repeat, list-removal, and store-path replacement cases.
- Confirmed the live system still has no system Flatpak overrides; the new
  activation has not been applied with `nixos-rebuild switch`.

### Status
- Implementation is complete and evaluated. No live rebuild or commit was made.
